From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 14:27:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/29] The packaging workflow, and the three bugs testing it
 found in itself

The previous commit's message described `.github/workflows/mali-driver-package.yml` and a
`EXPECTED_BACKEND_SHA256` gate in build.sh. Neither existed: the file was never written, and a patch
script that was silently skipped by an `&&` after a `wc` on a path /tmp had already stopped
containing. Both are here now, and the commit message before this one stays wrong - the tree is the
record, which is why docs/progress-log.md carries the correction too.

Writing the workflow properly meant running it locally, step for step, and the pipeline was broken in
three ways that each would have cost a CI run to find:

  - `zip -r "$OUT/$NAME" .` ran inside `$STAGE`, so a relative OUT (what CI passes) landed on a
    directory that did not exist there. OUT is resolved to an absolute path first.
  - the import-policy check compared `licenses/` against basenames, which never match - so it would
    have failed every green package and taught everyone to ignore it. It reads the raw namelist now,
    and also runs testzip().
  - the digest gate was added by a script that never executed. Reapplied, and tested both ways: a
    wrong digest exits 1 before anything is copied, the real rc2 digest passes.

Steps: resolve upstream's newest release through the API (/releases/latest is useless here - every
release upstream has made is a prerelease, so it 404s), download the -EMULATOR.zip plus SHA256SUMS
and check just that file, apt the AArch64 cross compiler, clone Vulkan-Headers, run the adapter test
suite, build, re-run the phone's own three import rules against the artifact, publish to a rolling
mali-driver-latest. No schedule, on purpose: this cannot mint a new renderer, so a weekly run would
re-publish identical bytes and look like progress.

docs/gpu-safety.md gained the section the last commit claimed for it, with the verified facts only:
the backend's SHA-256 equals upstream's published digest for rc2's glibc build, the notes say that
binary has never run on a device, and nothing in them mentions this guest's display server. dist/ is
gitignored, since a package is a release asset and never a tree entry.

